WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.519
So actinium: it’s often said about radioactivity
that you might be glowing

00:00:05.519 --> 00:00:09.880
in the dark if you work with radioactive elements.
By-and-large it’s not

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.269
true. However, actinium is one of them which
might do that because the

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:16.430
element itself glows blue in the dark.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:21.280
And actinium is actually more dangerous than
plutonium, and plutonium

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.490
was once described as the most deadly and
toxic substance known to

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.480
Man. So it’s pretty dangerous.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:31.680
I’ll avoid it! Have you had anything to
do with it at all?

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.890
No, because it’s so dangerous.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:40.909
When I was an undergraduate I saw this book
here, which was written

00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:45.510
by Seaborg, and I really liked the cover because
I think it looked really

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:50.199
nice and I was also quite interested about
the transuranium elements

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:55.629
because I didn’t really know much about
them. And he, probably more

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.299
than anybody else, discovered more of these
elements than any other

00:01:00.299 --> 00:01:06.210
scientist, probably more elements than most
if not all scientists. And he

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:11.710
wrote in this book about the chemistry of
the different elements and,

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.970
although we consider that these are pretty
radioactive and so on, they at

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:21.010
least managed to isolate enough material so
you could see it in the

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:25.800
bottom of a small glass tube. It may not have
been bigger than the tip of

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.780
a pin or something like that, but it’s a
huge amount compared to the one

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:34.950
or two atoms that were formed of some of these
elements very near the

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:35.970
end of the periodic table.

